.TH FEGETROUND 3 "2026-05-03" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
fegetround, fesetround \- get and set the floating-point rounding mode
.SH LIBRARY
Math Library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fenv.h>
.PP
.B "int fegetround(void);"
.BI "int fesetround(int " rdir );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR fegetround ()
and
.BR fesetround ()
functions query and set the current floating-point rounding direction.
.SS fegetround
.BR fegetround ()
returns the rounding direction macro corresponding to the current rounding
mode.
The default mode after program startup is
.BR FE_TONEAREST .
.SS fesetround
.BR fesetround ()
sets the floating-point rounding mode to
.IR rdir .
The argument must be one of the rounding mode macros defined in
.IR <fenv.h> :
.TP
.B FE_TONEAREST
Round to the nearest representable value; ties are broken by rounding to even
(the default IEEE 754 mode).
.TP
.B FE_DOWNWARD
Round toward negative infinity (floor rounding).
.TP
.B FE_UPWARD
Round toward positive infinity (ceiling rounding).
.TP
.B FE_TOWARDZERO
Round toward zero (truncation).
.PP
On x86, the rounding bits are stored in both the x87 control word (bits 10:11)
and, when SSE is available, in the MXCSR register (bits 13:14).
.BR fesetround ()
updates both.
.PP
Changing the rounding mode affects all subsequent floating-point operations
in the calling thread until the mode is changed again.
To limit the scope of a rounding-mode change, save the current mode with
.BR fegetround ()
and restore it afterward:
.PP
.nf
int saved = fegetround();
fesetround(FE_UPWARD);
/* ... operations under FE_UPWARD ... */
fesetround(saved);
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR fegetround ()
returns the current rounding mode macro
.RB ( FE_TONEAREST ,
.BR FE_DOWNWARD ,
.BR FE_UPWARD ,
or
.BR FE_TOWARDZERO )
on success, or a negative value if the current rounding mode cannot be
determined.
.PP
.BR fesetround ()
returns zero on success.
A non-zero return value indicates that
.I rdir
is not a valid rounding-mode macro or is not supported by the implementation.
.SH ERRORS
No errors are defined.
Neither function sets
.IR errno .
.SH EXAMPLES
The following example saves the current rounding mode, switches to
.BR FE_UPWARD ,
performs a computation, and then restores the original mode:
.PP
.nf
#pragma STDC FENV_ACCESS ON
#include <fenv.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    /* Save the current rounding mode. */
    int saved_rounding = fegetround();

    /* Switch to round-toward-positive-infinity. */
    if (fesetround(FE_UPWARD) != 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "fesetround: unsupported mode\\n");
        return 1;
    }

    volatile double a = 1.0, b = 3.0;
    volatile double result = a / b;   /* rounded up */
    printf("1.0 / 3.0 (FE_UPWARD) = %.17g\\n", result);

    /* Restore the original rounding mode. */
    fesetround(saved_rounding);

    return 0;
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fenv (7),
.BR fegetenv (3)
